{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Haṭu.</b> From your place, come to me, like 'turn to me, do not fear' (Judges 4:18)." "<b>And your soul shall live.</b> The meaning is the abiding of the soul at the death of the body. Or the meaning is that the Messiah shall give life, after they return to the religion of HaShem." "<b>And I will cut for you an everlasting covenant.</b> That shall not be shaken, like the covenant and kindness that I cut with David, as it is written, 'forever I shall keep my kindness for him' (Psalms 89:29). And it is possible that 'the kindnesses of David' is an allusion to the Messiah, who is of his family, just as the prophet is called Israel, and as though he is he, for the kindnesses of David are faithful, and the witness to this is the verse that follows it."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 55, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:59:36.206971673Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}